{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01JYtv3dq4FMhefg3tkttusC","type":"tool_result","content":"10\t\n11\tuse std::ffi::c_char;\n12\tuse std::ffi::c_int;\n13\t\n14\textern \"C\" {\n15\t    // Use the platform's printf so that formatting *and* stdio buffering\n16\t    // semantics are byte-for-byte identical to the C library's output.\n17\t    fn printf(fmt: *const c_char, ...) -> c_int;\n18\t\n19\t    // Use the platform's `strcspn` rather than reimplementing it.\n20\t    //\n21\t    // This is deliberate, and it is required for behavioural equivalence — a\n22\t    // hand-rolled `strcspn` is NOT a drop-in substitute even though it agrees\n23\t    // on every well-formed input:\n24\t    //\n25\t    //   * The obvious portable implementation scans `s1` in the outer loop and\n26\t    //     `s2` in the inner loop, so it never touches `s2` when `s1` is empty.\n27\t    //     glibc instead materialises the reject set from `s2` up front, so\n28\t    //     `driver(\"\", NULL)` faults in C but would quietly print `0` with a\n29\t    //     hand-rolled version. Argument-inspection order is observable here.\n30\t    //   * glibc's vectorised path over-reads within the current page and\n31\t    //     dispatches on the length of `s2` and on pointer alignment; the exact\n32\t    //     byte at which an unterminated buffer faults follows from that, not\n33\t    //     from a naive byte-at-a-time scan.\n34\t    //\n35\t    // Calling the same libc routine the C calls makes all of this identical by\n36\t    // construction instead of by imitation.\n37\t    fn strcspn(s1: *const c_char, s2: *const c_char) -> usize;\n38\t}\n39\t\n40\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n41\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn driver(s1: *const c_char, s2: *const c_char) {\n42\t    printf(b\"%zu\\n\\0\".as_ptr() as *const c_char, strcspn(s1, s2));\n43\t}\n44\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05b246d6-3983-4880-8482-a29663d135bf","uuid":"e00d66b6-123c-4fad-a527-39089b2b51