{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_2eekh2elineydeyxvka6ofl3vaui27eefdlyz2sl4uo2wzby4usq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **One real bug found and fixed.**\n\n## The bug\n\nThe translation hand-rolled `strcspn` with the natural loop nesting — `s1` outer, `s2` inner — so it never dereferences `s2` when `s1` is empty. glibc materializes the reject set from `s2` *first*:\n\n| input | C | Rust (before) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `driver(\"\", NULL)` | SIGSEGV | printed `0`, exit 0 |\n\nFixed by importing libc's `strcspn` via `extern \"C\"` instead of reimplementing it, making argument-inspection order, SIMD dispatch, and page-crossing fault behavior identical by construction. `src/lib.rs` net change: 30 lines of reimplementation → 1 `extern` declaration.\n\n## Phase artifacts (in `translation/`)\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C exports exactly 1 symbol (`driver`); Rust exports it. Diff empty in both profiles. No C module was skipped (the library is one function).\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — the C has *zero* explicit rejections (no `return`, `assert`, or range check), so the error surface is its 11 distinct fault behaviors. Each is asserted on **exact signal number** (SIGSEGV/11), not \"both failed.\"\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 28 rows over the axes the code actually branches on: reject-set size, match position, `strlen` across every SIMD block size, byte domain, alignment cross-product, buffer aliasing, page boundaries, printf digit widths.\n\n## Results\n\n324 test executions green: 54 tests × 6 configurations (default / no-default / all-features × debug / release). No `[features]` table exists, so those are the complete configuration space. No binary target exists in either build, so that gate is vacuous — but stdout is compared byte-for-byte on every row regardless, since it's `driver`'s only observable effect.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n**The suite was validated by mutation testing** (`scripts/mutation_check.sh`), because a green differential suite proves nothing unless it can fail. Notably, **Mutant A — the original buggy translation — passes all 28 Phase B rows** and is caught only by `ERRORS.md` row 2. Happy-path testing at any input volume would have shipped this bug.\n\n**Two harness bugs I hit that would have caused false passes**, both fixed:\n- `cargo test` does not build `cdylib` targets, so the debug run was silently loading the *release* `.so`. `verify_all.sh` now runs `cargo build` per profile first.\n- Piping `cargo test` into `grep` makes the pipeline exit status grep's, which matches almost any output — turning failures into reported PASSes. Now captured to a file with cargo's own status checked.\n\nStdout capture redirects process-wide fd 1, so tests run serially (`.cargo/config.toml` sets `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1`, with a mutex as backup); faulting cases run in isolated re-exec'd child processes with unbuffered stdout so pre-fault output can't hide in a buffer. `c_src/` is unmodified apart from the `build/` directory the task asked me to create."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":794,"cache_read_input_tokens":102327,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":794,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05b246d6-3983-4880-8482-a29663d135bf","uuid":"6d1aac3e-efc4-4034-94d9-5f2f026d23be","timestamp":"2026-09-05T05:02:18.037Z"}
